{0 Combinators}

Combinators compose simple parsers into more complex ones. They handle
alternation, error labeling, lookahead, and recursion.

{1 Alternation}

{2 [or_]}

{!val:Parseff.or_} tries the left parser. If it fails, backtracks (resets the
cursor) and tries the right parser.

{@ocaml skip[val or_ : (unit -> 'a) -> (unit -> 'a) -> unit -> 'a]}

{@ocaml[
let bool_parser () =
  Parseff.or_
    (fun () ->
      let _ = Parseff.consume "true" in
      true)
    (fun () ->
      let _ = Parseff.consume "false" in
      false)
    ()
(* "true"  -> Ok true  *)
(* "false" -> Ok false *)
(* "maybe" -> Error { pos = 0; error = `Expected "expected \"false\" or \"true\"" } *)
]}

On ["maybe"], both branches fail at the same position (position 0). Parseff
merges what each branch expected into one sorted list: {b expected "false" or
"true"}. The order of the branches does not change the message.

If the branches fail at {e different} positions, the parse reports the failure
that reached the furthest position. On ["tr"] the left branch fails at the end
of the input, which is further than the mismatch of the right branch, so the
error is [`Unexpected_end_of_input] at position 2.

A failure is recorded when it happens, not when the parse gives up. A branch that
fails and is abandoned still counts if it went further than anything else, even
when a later alternative succeeds.

[or_] is ideal for two alternatives. When you have more, use
{!val:Parseff.one_of}, which takes a list and avoids nested [or_] calls:

{@ocaml[
let keyword_with_or () =
  Parseff.or_
    (fun () -> Parseff.consume "let")
    (fun () ->
      Parseff.or_
        (fun () -> Parseff.consume "const")
        (fun () -> Parseff.consume "var")
        ())
    ()

let keyword_with_one_of () =
  Parseff.one_of
    [
      (fun () -> Parseff.consume "let");
      (fun () -> Parseff.consume "const");
      (fun () -> Parseff.consume "var");
    ]
    ()
]}

{2 [one_of]}

{!val:Parseff.one_of} tries each parser in order until one succeeds. Equivalent
to chaining [or_], but cleaner for more than two alternatives.

{@ocaml skip[val one_of : (unit -> 'a) list -> unit -> 'a]}

{@ocaml skip[
let json_value () =
  Parseff.one_of
    [
      null_parser;
      bool_parser;
      number_parser;
      string_parser;
      array_parser;
      object_parser;
    ]
    ()
]}

Error composition follows the same rules as [or_]: if all parsers fail at the
same position, what they expected is merged into one sorted list without
duplicates: [expected "else", "if", or "while"]. With three or more items the
list ends with [", or"]. If they fail at different positions, the failure that
progressed furthest wins.

{2 [one_of_labeled]}

{!val:Parseff.one_of_labeled} is like {!val:Parseff.one_of}, but each parser has
a label. On failure, the error message reports all labels, sorted:

{@ocaml skip[val one_of_labeled : (string * (unit -> 'a)) list -> unit -> 'a]}

{@ocaml skip[
let literal () =
  Parseff.one_of_labeled
    [
      ("number", fun () -> Number (Parseff.digit ()));
      ("string", string_parser);
      ("boolean", bool_parser);
    ]
    ()
(* On failure: "expected boolean, number, or string" *)
]}

This gives users a clear picture of what was expected at a given position,
without exposing internal parser details.

Each branch is wrapped by [expect]: only failures at the position where
[one_of_labeled] started are replaced by the label. If a branch fails further
in, for example an unterminated string, its own message is kept, because it
describes the problem better than ["string"].

{2 [optional]}

{!val:Parseff.optional} tries the parser. Returns [Some result] on success,
[None] on failure (without consuming input).

{@ocaml skip[val optional : (unit -> 'a) -> unit -> 'a option]}

{@ocaml skip[
let signed_number () =
  let sign = Parseff.optional (fun () -> Parseff.char '-') () in
  let n = number () in
  match sign with
  | Some _ -> -n
  | None -> n
(* "42"  -> 42  *)
(* "-42" -> -42 *)
]}

{1 Lookahead}

{2 [look_ahead]}

{!val:Parseff.look_ahead} runs a parser without consuming input. The cursor
stays where it was before the call. Fails if the parser fails.

{@ocaml skip[val look_ahead : (unit -> 'a) -> 'a]}

{@ocaml skip[
let peek_open_paren () =
  let _ = Parseff.look_ahead (fun () -> Parseff.char '(') in
  (* cursor hasn't moved, '(' is still the next character *)
  parse_parenthesized_expr ()
]}

Useful for context-sensitive decisions: peek at what's coming, then choose which
parser to run.

{1 Error labeling}

{2 [expect]}

{!val:Parseff.expect} runs a parser and relabels its failure with a clearer
message. The error reads [expected <description>].

{@ocaml skip[val expect : string -> (unit -> 'a) -> 'a]}

{@ocaml[
let dot () =
  Parseff.expect "a dot separator" (fun () -> Parseff.char '.')

let digit_val () = Parseff.expect "a digit (0-9)" Parseff.digit
]}

Use [expect] at grammar boundaries where user-facing wording is better than raw
token names:

{@ocaml skip[
let ip_address () =
  let a = number () in
  let _ = Parseff.expect "a dot separator" (fun () -> Parseff.char '.') in
  let b = number () in
  let _ = Parseff.expect "a dot separator" (fun () -> Parseff.char '.') in
  let c = number () in
  let _ = Parseff.expect "a dot separator" (fun () -> Parseff.char '.') in
  let d = number () in
  Parseff.end_of_input ();
  (a, b, c, d)
]}

Without [expect], a failed [char '.'] reports [expected '.']. With [expect], it
reports [expected a dot separator].

[expect] relabels only failures at the position where the parser started. In
[expect "key" (fun () -> consume "id:"; digit ())], the input ["foo"] reports
[expected key], but ["id:x"] reports [expected digit] at offset 3, because the
parser got past ["id:"] and the digit message is more precise.

{1 Naming constructs}

{2 [context]}

{!val:Parseff.context} runs a parser inside a named construct. The name does not
change what the parser accepts. A failure that happens inside it remembers which
constructs were open, outermost first, so an error report can say
"while parsing array".

{@ocaml skip[val context : ?delimited:bool -> string -> (unit -> 'a) -> 'a]}

{@ocaml[
let array_parser value () =
  Parseff.context ~delimited:true "array" (fun () ->
      let _ = Parseff.char '[' in
      let xs =
        Parseff.sep_by value
          (fun () ->
            Parseff.skip_whitespace ();
            Parseff.char ',')
          ()
      in
      Parseff.skip_whitespace ();
      let _ = Parseff.char ']' in
      xs)
]}

Use [~delimited:true] for constructs that have an opening token, such as an
array or a block. A parser that never calls [context] pays nothing for it. Each
call costs a few dozen instructions, so name constructs, not single tokens.

If two failures tie at the furthest position, the construct list of the first
one is kept.

{2 [cut]}

{!val:Parseff.cut} declares that the construct that is running is decided.
{!val:Parseff.commit} seals the nearest frame only. [cut] seals every [or_],
[one_of], [many], [sep_by], [optional] and [fold_*] frame that was opened since
the innermost enclosing [context]. It stops at {!val:Parseff.look_ahead}.

{@ocaml skip[val cut : unit -> unit]}

If the enclosing [context] then fails, the frames outside it are sealed too, so
an enclosing [or_], [many] or [sep_by] cannot swallow the failure:

{@ocaml[
let statement () =
  Parseff.context "statement" (fun () ->
      Parseff.or_
        (fun () ->
          let _ = Parseff.consume "let" in
          Parseff.cut ();
          Parseff.skip_whitespace ();
          let _ = Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 ~label:"name" (fun c -> c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') in
          Parseff.skip_whitespace ();
          let _ = Parseff.char '=' in
          Parseff.skip_whitespace ();
          let _ = Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 ~label:"expression" (fun c -> c >= '0' && c <= '9') in
          Parseff.char ';')
        (fun () ->
          let _ = Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 (fun c -> c >= '0' && c <= '9') in
          Parseff.char ';')
        ())

let statements () = Parseff.many (fun () -> Parseff.skip_whitespace (); statement ()) ()
(* "let x = 1; 2;"  -> Ok [';'; ';'] *)
(* "let x = ;"      -> Error at 8: expected expression *)
]}

With [commit ()] in place of [cut ()], only the [or_] is sealed, [many] stops
and [statements] returns [[]] without reading the rest of the input. With
[cut ()], the failure of [let x = ;] is the error of the parse.

[cut] also helps streaming: sealed frames no longer pin buffered input. See
{!page:streaming}.

{1 Recursion}

{2 [rec_]}

{!val:Parseff.rec_} marks a recursive entry point for depth tracking. Wrap the
body of recursive parsers with [rec_] so that {!val:Parseff.parse} can enforce
[max_depth] and fail cleanly instead of overflowing the stack.

{@ocaml skip[val rec_ : (unit -> 'a) -> 'a]}

{@ocaml skip[
let rec json () =
  Parseff.rec_ (fun () ->
    Parseff.skip_whitespace ();
    Parseff.one_of
      [
        array_parser;
        object_parser;
        null_parser;
        bool_parser;
        number_parser;
        string_parser;
      ]
      ()
  )

and array_parser () =
  let _ = Parseff.consume "[" in
  (* ... calls json () recursively ... *)
  let _ = Parseff.consume "]" in
  Array elements
]}

The [~max_depth] parameter on {!val:Parseff.parse} controls the limit (default:
128):

{@ocaml skip[
(* Reject inputs nested deeper than 64 levels *)
Parseff.parse ~max_depth:64 input json
]}

When the limit is exceeded, parsing fails with
["maximum nesting depth N exceeded"] rather than a stack overflow.

{b Tip:} Only wrap the top-level recursive entry point with [rec_]. You don't
need it on every mutually recursive function, just the one that represents
"entering a new nesting level".
